    text cue1 "a pay cut on what you buy" at.data [2023.95,6.95] font_size 32 hidden true
    text cue2 "eggs vs. everything else" #2f6b8f at.data [2023.95,6.3] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Eggs at six dollars"

How do you choose, when your money runs out before your wishes do? Start small: the price of a dozen eggs in America.
    draw axes

In January 20 (@draw p22 p22_label@)22 a dozen cost under two dollars. Then bird flu swept throu (@draw flu@)gh the hen houses, and millions of laying hens were culled.

    ask "Draw your guess for the price of a dozen eggs, from 2022 on." on line_1 from 2022 store g relative true voice b
        intro: So where did the price go next?
        right: Close: on average you were {g.pct} off.
        wrong: {g.pct} off on average: the real line is wilder than most guesses.

Up to four dollars (@draw p23 p23_label@) eighty-two in January 2023.

Back down to about two. Then a record six (@draw p25 p25_label@) twenty-three in March 2025.

B: Three times the price. So every family got poorer by the same amount?
    highlight p25 effect circle

No. A family that never buys eggs lost nothing at all. One that (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@) buys two dozen a week paid over ei (@draw calc_line_2@)ght dollars more.

B: So  (@erase calc flu@)a price rise is (@draw cue1@) a pay cut, but only on the things you buy.

And it does something a pay cut doesn't: it chang (@draw cue2@)es what eggs cost compared with everything else.

To see both at once, we draw everything a budget can buy. Its edge has a name: the budget line.
    highlight line_1

    pause 0.4

## Slide or tilt
vars: {"inc":40}
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 40
    price_x: 2
    price_y: 4
    x_max: 36
    y_max: 16
    curves: 1
    show_optimum: false
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    x_good: Eggs
    y_good: Bread
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 130
      w: 700
      h: 525

    text inc "${inc} a week" x 840 y 630 font_size 38 hidden true
    text bl_text "budget line" at.data [13,6] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot bread_end at.data [0,10] hidden true
    text bread_end_label "10 loaves" at.data [4,11] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot egg_end at.data [20,0] hidden true
    text egg_end_label "20 dozen" at.data [22.5,1.4] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot egg_end2 #b5482e at.data [8,0] hidden true
    text egg_end2_label "8 dozen" #b5482e at.data [10.5,1.4] font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon set points [[0,0],[0,10],[20,0]] data true style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.35 style.stroke none hidden true
    text set_label "affordable" at.data [5.5,3] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch calc work ["$40 ÷ $4 = 10","$40 ÷ $2 = 20","slope: $2 ÷ $4 = ½"] x 830 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch calc2 work ["slope: $5 ÷ $4","= 1¼"] x 830 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work [{"tex":"P_x x + P_y y = I"},{"tex":"\\text{ends: } I/P_y,\\ I/P_x"},{"tex":"\\text{slope} = -P_x/P_y"}] x 830 y 330 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Slide or tilt"

    hide ic_1 egg_end2 egg_end2_label

B: So we need the whole budget on one page. Take a Harbourtown family with, say, forty dolla (@draw inc@)rs a week for bread and eggs.
    draw axes

B: Mia's bread costs four dollars a loaf, and egg (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@)s two dollars a dozen. Spend it all on bre (@draw bread_end bread_end_label@)ad: ten loaves.

And all (@draw calc_line_2@) on eggs, twent (@draw egg_end egg_end_label@)y dozen. Nobody would, but it marks the other end.

B: Join the tw (@draw budget_line@)o ends. Every point on thi (@draw bl_text@)s line spends exactly forty dollars.

And inside the line?
    point at.data [6,3] gesture circle

B: Money left over. Ev (@draw set set_label@)erything in this triangle is affordable; everything beyond it is out of reach.

B: The slope is the price o (@draw calc_line_3@)f eggs, counted  (@highlight budget_line@)in bread: each dozen costs half a loaf.

Say the family gets a raise, to sixty dollars a week.
    erase calc set set_label bl_text

B: Both ends move out by half. The line slides out, and its slope doesn't change at all.
    animate income 60 inc 60 duration 3 ghost true

Back  (@animate income 40 inc 40 duration 1.5@)to forty. Now eggs jump to five dollars a dozen.
    erase budget_line_ghost

    ask "Eggs go from $2 to $5 a dozen. Which end of the line moves? Tap it." choose ["bread_end","egg_end"] answer egg_end voice b
        right: The egg end: ten loaves still cost forty dollars.
        wrong: Ten loaves still cost forty dollars, so the bread end stays: here, the egg end.

B: Only the egg end moves. Forty dollars buys just eight doz (@erase egg_end egg_end_label@)en now (@draw egg_end2 egg_end2_label@), but still ten loaves, so the line swings round the bread end.
    animate price_x 5 duration 3 ghost true

So a price change tilts  (@draw calc2_box calc2_line_1@)the line. Each dozen now costs o (@draw calc2_line_2@)ne and a quarter loaves.

    erase calc2 budget_line_ghost_2

B: Try it yourself: drag the egg end, then the middle of the line, and watch which end stays put.
    explore

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a one-line equation behind all this.

@math

    erase mathq

Pric (@draw m_box m_line_1@)e of eggs times eggs, plus price of bread times bread, equals the budget.

B: Spend it all on one good (@draw m_line_2@), and the end is the budget divided by that good's price.

Rearrange, and the slope (@draw m_line_3@) is minus one price over the other. Income isn't in it.

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

Income slides the line; a price tilts it, because the slope is one price divided by the other.
    highlight budget_line

    pause 0.5

## A dollar is not a dollar
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Your budget line is in excellent shape.
    good: Mostly straight lines. Nicely done.
    poor: 'Slide, tilt, or stay: worth one more look.'
    none: The line is still there, waiting for you.
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 1000
    price_x: 1
    price_y: 1
    x_max: 1200
    y_max: 1200
    curves: 1
    show_optimum: false
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    x_good: Food ($)
    y_good: Other things ($ a month)
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 140
      w: 660
      h: 495
sources:
    - id: usda_snap
      title: 'Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP): key statistics'
      authors: USDA Economic Research Service
      year: 2025
      finding: SNAP served an average of 41.7 million people a month in fiscal year 2024
      url: https://www.ers.usda.gov/topics/food-nutrition-assistance/supplemental-nutrition-assistance-program-snap/key-statistics-and-research
    - id: hastings2018
      title: How Are SNAP Benefits Spent? Evidence from a Retail Panel
      authors: Hastings, J. & Shapiro, J. M.
      year: 2018
      finding: The marginal propensity to consume SNAP-eligible food out of SNAP benefits is 0.5 to 0.6, far above that out of cash, even for households whose benefits are below their food spending; fungibility is rejected, consistent with mental accounting
      doi: 10.1257/aer.20170866
    - id: hoynes2009
      title: 'Consumption Responses to In-Kind Transfers: Evidence from the Introduction of the Food Stamp Program'
      authors: Hoynes, H. W. & Schanzenbach, D. W.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'As the Food Stamp Program rolled out county by county in the 1960s and early 1970s, households responded to food stamps about as they would to the same amount of cash: they behaved as inframarginal'

    text snap "SNAP: 42 million people" cites ["usda_snap"] x 830 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cash_text "all cash" at.data [760,340] font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon cut points [[0,700],[300,700],[0,1000]] data true style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke none hidden true
    path kink points [[0,700],[300,700],[1000,0]] data true style.stroke #2f6b8f style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text cutlbl "cash only" #b5482e at.data [360,930] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot fam at.data [450,550] hidden true
    text famlbl "this family" at.data [640,600] font_size 28 hidden true
    scale guess cites ["hastings2018"] x 640 y 440 width 320 font_size 28 label "food per $100 SNAP" min 0 max 100 value 55 markers [{"value":10,"label":"theory"}] hidden true
    text hs "Hastings & Shapiro 2018" cites ["hastings2018"] x 840 y 360 font_size 24 hidden true
    text env "mental envelopes" #b5482e x 835 y 290 font_size 30 hidden true
    text hoynes "1960s–70s: like cash" cites ["hoynes2009"] x 835 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text st1 "budget line: consensus" #2f6b8f x 835 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text st2 "every dollar alike: contested" #b5482e x 835 y 410 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "A dollar is not a dollar"

    hide ic_1

A straight line assumes a dollar is a dollar, whatever you spend it on. Some budgets break that on purpose.
    draw axes

B: Like America's SNAP, the old food stamps.  (@draw snap@)About forty-two million people get money that can only buy food.

Say a family has seven hundred dollars  (@draw budget_line@)in cash and three hundred in SNAP a month. A thousand in ca (@draw cash_text@)sh would give this straight line.

B: With SNAP, the t (@fade budget_line cash_text to 0.3@)op is cut flat: f (@draw kink@)ood money can't pay the rent, so (@draw cut cutlbl@) at most seven hundred goes on other things.

But thi (@erase snap@)s family spends more than three hundred on food anyw (@draw fam famlbl@)ay. For them the missing corner doesn't matter: SNAP is as good as cash.

B: So theory predicts they spend an extra SNA (@draw guess@)P dollar like an extra cash dollar, and typical estimates say about ten cents of that goes on food.

    ask "Out of $100 more SNAP, how many dollars went on extra food? Click the line." on guess store g default "30" voice b
        right: You said {g}; the receipts say about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; the receipts say about {g.true}.

Justine Hasti (@draw hs@)ngs and Jesse Shapiro followed shoppers' receipts. Of each ext (@highlight guess@)ra SNAP dollar, fifty to sixty cents went on food.

B: Even in families where SNAP was just like cash. They seem to k (@draw env@)eep food money in its own mental envelope.

Yet when food stamps first rolled out, in the 1960s and 70s, Hilary Ho (@draw hoynes@)ynes and Diane Schanzenbach found families spent them much like cash.
    erase guess env hs

B: So how  (@erase hoynes@)sure are we? That the budget line is the (@draw st1@) edge of what you can buy: consensus. That people treat eve (@draw st2@)ry dollar alike: contested.

    quiz "Your income doubles, and so does every price. What happens to your budget line?"
        right: It doesn't move: each end is income over a price, and both double.
        wrong: Each end is income over a price: here, it doesn't move.
        * It moves out
        + It doesn't move
        * It tilts

    quiz "A sales tax raises every price by 25%; your pay stays the same. What does your budget line do?" voice b
        right: Slides inward: both ends shrink by a fifth, and one price over the other is unchanged.
        wrong: Does one price over the other change? Here: slides inward.
        * Tilts toward the cheaper good
        + Slides inward, same slope
        * Doesn't move

    quiz "A family would spend about $100 a month on childcare, even with more money. They get a $300 voucher that only pays for childcare. On the budget line alone, how does it compare with $300 in cash?"
        right: Worth less: the voucher forces at least $300 onto childcare, so they're stuck on the cut corner that cash would let them leave.
        wrong: Does the voucher stop a bundle cash would allow? Here: worth less.
        * Worth the same
        + Worth less
        * Worth more

So how do you choose with a limited budget? First find the edge: income sets how far out it lies, prices set its tilt.
    highlight kink

Which point on it you pick depends on what you want, and that needs a picture of its own.
    highlight fam effect circle

    pause 0.5

## Where next
